// The theme, and the one line that chooses it.
//
// Everything the interface is made of — every colour, every radius, every
// metric, every step of the type scale — lives in the palette this file
// re-exports, and nowhere else. Nothing outside this directory names a colour.
// So retheming the app is editing one file, and shipping a second theme is
// writing a second one and changing the line below.
//
//     export { Theme } from "dark.slint";
//
// To fork the look: copy `dark.slint`, change its values, point this at it.
// The palette's own header says what each group is for and what the rules
// between them are — which surface floats on which, what may sit on the
// accent, why the lanes are darker than the pane they are in. A palette that
// keeps those relationships will look like a theme; one that only swaps hues
// will look like a recolour.
//
// Two things are deliberately *not* here:
//
//   - The effect previews in ui/assets, which are photographs of what an
//     effect does rather than anything a theme decides.
//   - Anything the *document* is made of that is not also a colour choice —
//     a clip's own text, its opacity, a fade's length. The colours a title
//     can be *are* here, under `content`, because a fork that retones the
//     interface will want the shelf beside it to agree.
//
// There is one palette today. The seam is the point: a light theme is a file
// next to this one, not a rewrite of forty others.
export { Theme } from "dark.slint";
